abstract: >-
  Quantum Spectrodynamics (QSD) is a framework for deriving physical
  dynamics from quantum error correction. From a single structural axiom --
  the universe implements a quantum error-correcting code [[n,k,d]]_q with
  all parameters initially unknown -- the perfect five-qubit code [[5,1,3]]
  is selected (seven-step exclusion chain plus Landauer's principle), and
  its geometry fixes three constants: the holographic bit density
  Hb_4 = 1/(4 ln 2) (from K4), the spectral bond varpi = pi*phi^2 (from
  C5), and the geometric strain sigma = 3/(80 phi^2) (from their union).
  With zero free parameters beyond the Planck mass these yield the gauge
  couplings, particle masses, and cosmological constant to 0.004%-1.2%
  across six observables, plus the falsifiable 28.24 GeV snap-energy
  prediction. This modular record grows by parts: v1.0 is the Standard
  Model sector (main preprint + supplements S1-S7 + TN-1); v2.0 adds
  Phase 2 Paper 1 (the QECC Selection Theorem) with supplements S8-S9.
